## SECTION 5: AUDIENCE INTELLIGENCE
- who_they_are: True nail-art newcomers, self-identifying as having "no experience" or "not much experience"; often teens/young adults influenced by Pinterest/TikTok trends but with zero hands-on practice; some have shaky or unsteady hands and worry this disqualifies them entirely.
- what_they_want_from_this_post: A genuinely trustworthy starting point — designs confirmed easy by real beginners (not editorial "easy"), told in an order that builds confidence rather than risking early failure and giving up.
- what_frustrates_them: Being handed a "beginner" list that still assumes tools they don't own or a steadiness they don't have; vague instructions that skip why something goes wrong; roundups that don't explain nail prep at all, causing chipped/lifted results within a day.
- top_5_pain_points: (1) Flooding the cuticle with polish because they don't know to leave a gap. (2) Applying polish too thick, causing streaks, bubbling, and slow drying. (3) Not waiting long enough between coats before ruining the layer underneath. (4) Believing steady hands are a prerequisite, not a skill. (5) Not knowing which household item substitutes for which real tool (toothpick vs. bobby pin vs. tape).
- exact_user_phrases: "not much experience," "if you have patience you can compensate your lack of experience," "no skills needed, just hands," "impossible to mess up," "no steady hands needed," "every attempt looks intentional," "start with what you have," "don't try hard things, don't try detailed designs," "gorgeous nails don't happen by accident"
- intent_content_signals: Sequential step lists outperform prose descriptions; naming the specific mistake before the fix builds trust; explicit tool substitutions (toothpick = dotting tool) reduce bounce; short "why this works for beginners" reassurance lines after each design increase completion likelihood; video-style numbered steps (as seen dominating Pinterest) are the expected format even in text.

